[{:seon.task/name "main-wins-a-conflict"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: when an agent merges main (`seon.agent/merge-main!`) and both sides changed a key, `seon.merge/three-way` names the conflict, the branch keeps its own content, and the conflict stays an unmerged entry (`seon.merge/unmerged-tx`) until the branch redefines the key or resolves it (`seon.merge/resolve-tx`); docs/parity.md: ours is kept. v1 (MERGE-STEP 5a): main's content wins on the branch and the branch's own is kept on the entry (:seon.merge/kept), so the agent runs main's definition and re-applies its change knowingly. Correct: merge-main! applies theirs for a conflict too, the entry holds :seon.merge/kept, and `seon.merge/unmerged` keeps it open until the branch resolves it. The attribute's name waits on the owner's D6 ruling, shared with seon.merge/a-merge-task-carries-what-it-names."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-conflict-on-merging-main-runs-mains-definition-and-keeps-ours}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-task-carries-what-it-names"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a merge task merges main into the work branch whole (`seon.agent/merge-main!`) and the writer merges the whole branch, scratch helpers included. Owner, 2026-10-22: nothing moves between branches by default. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §3, prototyped: triple conflict, triples copy, nope missing, no scratch carried): :seon.task/carry ([:var sym] [:schema k] [:datum e a]), :seon.task/source, :seon.task/target; the merge branch starts at the target's head; each item judged against the merge base as missing, same, copy or conflict; a carried function's branch-only callees not carried are missing (v1: a reach-chosen list missed 4 chain-only helpers); a conflict entry records the side the branch holds (:seon.merge/kept) so `seon.merge/unmerged` stays open until resolved. Deletes: the merge task's whole-branch merge-main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-merge-task-judges-each-item seon.merge-test/an-uncarried-branch-only-callee-is-missing}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store" :seon.task/plan "Whether a Claude worktree branch can merge through the store (the design in docs/merging.md; the review docs/merging.md says: on hold until experiment 3's diff is empty). Measured (the scratch paper merge-probe-2026-10-27.md (git history); probe root on 4840ddb's first parent, jolt/data-attrs-from-the-index imported by `seon.source/refresh-tx`): experiment 1 holds on correctness: :ours of `seon.merge/delta-three-way` is exactly the definitions git's diff touches (10 vars, 16 forms), 0 conflicts, 67 datoms written for the branch, `seon.source/files` byte-equal to git show tip for both files; but it cost 12,607 calls and 277 MB, not about 39 and under 600 KB (main's side changed 79 keys since the base, and the values were d/db-with copies, not store branches: the number on real d/branch! branches is not measured). Experiment 2 fails: the merged program's 917 tests all plan to run, 0 reused from test-results/ that bin/prove --merge proved green; the first differing digest is datahike.query-test/a-nested-call-in-a-predicate-is-refused (import 11baa001 against 5 stored greens). Cause, partial: every digest takes :env, which digests `seon.fx/config-input`, the whole config in force less :seon.config/place keys; the probe root's config was not bin/test's (a --set of agent max-running, its own --config layer); restoring that key changed the digest and still left 917. Next probe: compare bin/test --plan's per-test digests on 4840ddb's tree with the import's, one by one. Experiment 3, not run: on a worktree root (its own store, never runs/store, :seon.config.source/branch set to a probe-only branch so write-back cannot touch jolt/merges or jolt/seon-merges), attach a task, hand back a past branch that touched src, test, default.edn and a bin script and deleted something, through seon_attach and seon_done; measure whether the judge ran on the imported code (the review: the agent's hand-back runs the proofs on its loaded program, so the import must be the agent's own, as ``seon.agent/load-tests!`` (since deleted) does, before hand-back), what the verdict tree held against git show tip (default.edn, bin and deps.edn are not in the store), and the write-back commit's tree diff against the branch tip, file modes included (`seon.fx/git-commit` writes every file 100644 and cannot delete). Routing Claude worktrees through the store waits until that diff is empty." :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/an-outside-branch-writes-back-its-own-tree}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-merge-system"
  :seon.task/plan "The open design (plan direction 3; this task holds docs/merging.md and its review docs/merging.md): one merge system from what Seon already has, not two. Facts (main 4840ddb153): since 2026-10-20 main took 301 first-parent merges, all by git (worktree merges and bin/prove --merge); Seon's own merge (octopus plus write-back) made 14 Seon merge commits, all only on jolt/seon-merges (the review: 0 in main). Duplications the audit named: two mains (git and the store, synced by seon.source/refresh-tx on watch and by write-back plus bin/update); two verdicts (the judge defers process tests and runs them only through my.root/gate!; bin/prove --merge runs every key with no green) and a third runner, seon.prove, the test service (the review: the design never says what becomes of it, and the :tests effect would need a new contract and a lock); two serializers (bin/env's flock and the reconciler's one merge flight); two records (facts and the merge log, now the merge commit message); a merge task per work task (done: a work task merges itself through the one gate, docs/merging.md); three branch-vs-base diffs (seon.merge/delta-changes, seon.program/divergence, seon.program/diff-plan, the last for loading). Owner rulings that bound it: Seon's merge path stays whole and is exercised before anything is cut (do not throw out the baby); a work task merges itself; worktrees retire. Review corrections to apply to the design before anything is built: octopus and merge-keys are not dead under max-running 1 (that caps only :merge-kind starts; whole tasks in one judge pass merge as one octopus), so cutting to one branch is a behaviour change; gate-checkout is set by no local.edn (the gate has never run on the standing root); the branch-key mismatch is worktree roots writing the shared ref (done: bin/env names jolt/seon-merges-<worktree>); the import must be the agent's own before hand-back (seon.agent/load-tests!), not root's after it; the verdict tree must be the branch's own tree because default.edn, system.edn, tasks, bin and deps.edn are not in the store; seon.fx/git-commit writes mode 100644 and cannot delete (a bin script loses its executable bit, deletions are routine), so pin bumps, native code and deletions stay git merges until it can; a Seon merge commit with no branch parent makes the branch look unmerged and drops its measured commit message; a synchronous seon_done running process tests on every merge overturns the 2026-10-23 process-tests ruling and needs the owner. Order: the deletions that stand alone first (done: file tasks merge themselves; the gate and :unrun problem and the merge-log as a render of facts remain); routing worktrees through the store waits on seon.merge/an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store (experiment 3). Correct: the design names which runner (seon.prove or bin/prove) is the one verdict and deletes the other's verdict role, and carries the two experiments' numbers. Proof to write: after the build, no second verdict path is left (a resolve of the gate's vars is nil) and one result store answers both bin/test and the judge (0 keys to run for a tree bin/prove --merge proved)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.merge/an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store"}}
 {:seon.task/name "what-merges-is-what-a-file-states"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: \"it should be the same sync system\"; \"we do need a one way overwrite on things. Is the wiki that different? Don't overthink this if there's a clear solution\"; \"we need to be clear in the diffs that agents are getting when we are merging from main and something is changing\". (A :seon.merge/carried kind on entity schemas was built and dropped: \"the carried type is a kind and you aren't supposed to do that\".) Found on md-scan 29: main moved its wiki under a living branch, merge-main carried the code and not the sections, and seon.problems/caused-split held its done on a catch main's wiki named.\n\nBuilt (3a69494970): a section is a program key, [:section address], in the one three-way beside [:schema k] (docs/merging.md#seons-merge-is-a-three-way-merge-of-the-program): main's change to a section the branch did not change overwrites it, one both changed is an unmerged entry, main's new and removed pages reach the branch; a branch's own section change is refused by the writer as :seon.merge/unwritten until pages are written back.\n\nLeft, in order: (1) an agent is told what came in: seon.turn/work!'s first merge-main! discards its answer today, and the merge's applied and conflicts reach no context; the agent's next request carries what main brought, grouped by kind (functions, schemas, wiki sections, task content), each a count and its names, and the form that reads one change as clojure.data/diff of base and main, a conflict with base, ours and theirs and the form that resolves it, drawn by the catch-up's declared model render, sent once; proof seon.agent-test/an-agent-is-told-what-came-in-from-main. (2) write-back: the writer writes a changed section back to its page and a changed task entry back to its task file, once each round-trips exactly (file, entities, file), and :seon.merge/unwritten goes; then seon.task/carry-tx folds into the three-way (task content a key like a section). (3) the data-attrs argument (root api :all, my.branch and the writer #{}) and seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs and platform-record? deleted: one merge carries one thing whoever asks.\n\nFor the owner, 2026-10-31 (the sessions' proposal, not yet approved): an agent cannot change the wiki at all today, which the owner's direction (agents improve the wiki through what they cite) needs. Found by the fence bench (bench-fence-1, 0 of 2): asked to make \"fence\" a library word in AGENTS.md section 7a, the agent asked at turn 7 whether AGENTS.md was tracked so it could edit it, nothing said how, and it looped 25 turns. What is missing, in order, each reusing what exists: (a) one documented page write for an agent, e.g. my.doc/write! of an address or path to its text, that loads it as seon.doc/load-tx does into the agent's branch (the section entities the three-way already merges; no file is touched), with a help-page section saying how; (b) write-back, step (2) above: the merges branch's :git effect writes each changed page back to docs/ as forms are written back to src/, round-tripping exactly; (c) the writer's :seon.merge/unwritten refusal goes once (b) holds; (d) code and tests that read the wiki read it from the branch's db, not the checkout's files, so an agent's change is judged where it was made. Then a wiki-edit bench task (the fence task, parked until (a)-(d)). Checked 2026-11-06: step (2) is built for pages: f39b5c3521 writes a merge's changed doc pages back (seon.source/changed-files, seon.doc/files, my.root/write-back!) and the :seon.merge/unwritten refusal is gone from src (proved by seon.merge-test/a-branch-doc-edit-merges-and-its-page-is-written-back, green; seon.merge-test/a-branch-takes-mains-wiki is green). Not built: step (1) (seon.agent-test/an-agent-is-told-what-came-in-from-main is not written), task entries written back to their files, step (3) (seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs and platform-record? exist), and my.doc/write! (src/my/doc.clj holds only reviewed!)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-branch-takes-mains-wiki seon.merge-test/a-branch-doc-edit-merges-and-its-page-is-written-back}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-takes-options"
  :seon.task/plan "Approved by the owner, 2026-11-08, with seon.merge/what-a-side-never-held-is-no-change, as the user-branch merges. Today the merge picks keys and conflict handling inside its callers. Correct: one function of target, source and base, each in `:seon.db/at-opts`' shape, never a db value, and options: which keys, `-X theirs` or `-X ours`, which data. A cherry-pick records no second parent and doesn't move the base. Proof, as in the lab: send one namespace of two to main, the other stays on the user's branch, a pull back loses nothing, and a whole merge later brings the rest; with the same var changed on both sides, the default leaves one unmerged entry, `-X theirs` leaves none and main's source. Out of scope: who calls it. See docs/users.md#the-merges."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.merge-test/a-cherry-pick-leaves-the-rest-to-merge-later seon.merge-test/x-theirs-takes-the-upstreams-value}}
 {:seon.task/name "what-a-side-never-held-is-no-change"
  :seon.task/plan "Approved by the owner, 2026-11-08, with seon.merge/a-merge-takes-options. Found 2026-11-07 on a scratch store through the real seon.merge (docs/sharing.md#where-a-fact-lives): s2 takes a program-only merge from U with U as a parent, so its merge base holds a datom D it never held; D then changes on U; s2's whole merge reports [:datum e a] :deleted-by-them, though s2 never had or deleted it. No data changes, but a person or agent is asked to settle nothing. Correct: a key the side lacks with no retraction in its history is no change of that side, as seon.merge/carried-tx already reads retractions. Proof, red first: that sequence reports no conflict; a real retraction on a session against a change on U still does. Needed when user branches take whole merges: no path today merges whole in that order."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-session-syncs-with-main-unless-private"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-09: \"I want agents to be able to get any data from any previous sessions ... the ability to specify if agent sessions should be 'private' (not auto syncing with main) or always syncing everything\"; ruled: \"The private session only takes from main what is on main at the time it is branched.\" Spec by Web (worked with Context and Optimization). What exists, read on main: an agent's own pulls of main pass data-attrs #{} (my.branch/merge-main!, seon.turn at a turn whose main moved), seon.root/request defaults :all (two defaults for one operation); merges up (the gate's :merge-candidate, the reconciler's octopus merge) pass #{}; and seon.merge/platform-record? keeps every seon.* attribute but carried-platform-attrs out of every merge, so an agent's evals, model calls, cites, searches and delivered messages never reach main, and main's status, model, max-turns and messages never reach a branch by merge. Nothing on main reads an agent's evals or model calls (measured 2026-11-09: the judge and problems read the task's cites; cross-agent reads go through the union).\n\nThe change:\n- One setting, data on the agent entity: ``:seon.agent/private true`` (absent: syncing; no kind).\n- Private: the branch is main as it was at the fork, and takes nothing from main after (program included); its data never goes up.\n- Syncing (the default): down, everything on main that does not conflict, data included, at each turn's open where :seon.agent/main-commit is written; up, everything it made that does not conflict, its work included, with conflicts kept as unmerged entries the agent resolves (seon.merge/unmerged-tx), as program conflicts are.\n- One down-merge default for every caller, taken from the setting; seon.root/request's own :all goes.\n- What may cross is decided by writer, not namespace: what root writes on main about an agent (status, process, boot, model, max-turns) is main's half and goes down only; what the agent writes on its branch is its work. carried-platform-attrs becomes a case of the rule. Content-id entities (results, messages, cites) never conflict.\n- Sharing: content-addressed nodes (docs/sharing.md#what-a-branchs-nodes-are) keep a syncing branch mostly main's nodes.\nMeasured 2026-11-09 (one run each, load 3-5): a merge is flat in main's size (main 7.6x, cost +3-6%); carrying data (:all) costs about 60k ticks a datom moved (merge-tx 7.5k, seon.merge/delta-three-way under :all about 44k: a cost to cut first), 3.0M ticks at 10 datoms, 62M and 153 MB at 1,000; program only 1.8-6.8M; one agent run wrote 432 datoms in 33 transactions (about 65 a turn), so an up-merge a turn is about 7M ticks at today's cost.\n\nSettled with the owner, 2026-11-09 (\"I do want options for agents to be private (experimental not syncing with main the entire time) and an auto sync feature (likely the default behavior) which is always trying to sync everything that we can except for the conflicts which they have to resolve\"; \"I like the idea of only syncing data that's not invalid. If we aren't testing something and there's no conflict I guess it just goes through\"; \"an agent can modify their own view, but they can't modify the whole system without an explicit save\"):\n- Syncing goes up at each turn's end, what it made since its last sync, less what is held: a name with an open unmerged entry, and a change a reached test holds red (the tests the gate runs today, seon.reconcile's done judgment; the write check already refuses an invalid value). A change no test reaches and no conflict touches goes through. What is held stays on the branch and goes up at the first sync after it is resolved or green.\n- Private goes up only when the person saves it: the save is a fact on the agent (desired state, as every request to the reconciler), and the reconciler merges the branch, held to the same rule, retrying as what held it changes; a save is also how a private session becomes a syncing one. Today's automatic merge of a green task goes: a private branch reaches main only by a save, a syncing one by its syncs.\n- Who sets private: the person, per session.\n- What holds a change is data the agent reads on its page: each unmerged entry (base, ours, theirs) and each hold reason (the red test and its first failure) is an entity, the entry with a ref to its branch (Optimization's re-model 3), each drawn by its schema's render in the agent's page parts, so the model reads it in each turn's diff and the person sees the same (Web writes the two renders). Today's main line on an outside agent's eval (seon.agent/main-line) becomes a reading of the same entities.\n\nProofs, red first: a syncing session's eval, cite and search reach main, and a session started after reads them; a private session takes nothing from main after its fork and nothing of it reaches main; root's half of an agent never merges up; a data conflict is an unmerged entry the agent resolves; merge-main! asked through root's api and from a turn carry the same data for one session. Cost proofs: a turn's up-merge costs the datoms it carries; delta-three-way under :all costs the datoms that differ, near merge-tx's 7.5k a datom.\n\nState, 2026-11-09 (Optimization): branch jolt/sync, worktree .worktrees/sync, head 40eb4f7b8b, not landed. Built and proved: one identity is one entity in a merge (seon.merge/delta-three-way keys a datum by its identity; merge-tx carries an identity entity by value and remaps refs; red first: Datahike's unique constraint threw); stored bytes compare by Datahike's datahike.datom/compare-value (seon.merge/same?; red: equal bytes conflicted); delta-three-way under :all cut from 56.4M to 16.9M ticks at 1,000 values (each key encoded once, contents read off the diff; 10.7M of the rest is Datahike's two datoms-diff); ``:seon.agent/private`` and one down-merge default from it; root writes an agent's status on main only at start, park and end (:seon.process/running gone; the page's running form from root's live view is owed to stage 4, from the agent's own render answer); a function's cost is its runs (``:seon.run/*`` entities, summed by ``seon.agent/fn-cost``); re-model 3 built (an unmerged entry refs its agent, ``:seon.merge/agent``; a branch is not an entity; seon.merge's entries, unmerged, unmerged-tx and resolve-tx take the agent's key; ctx/status gets one argument, Context told). Re-model 2, the owner's pick of the smaller design (2026-11-09): :seon.vector/of stays the identity, and ``:seon.vector/text``, the hash of the embedded text, replaces :seon.vector/text-t, so two branches embedding one text hold equal values (equal bytes merge as equal); a text is due when its hash differs; about 10 lines, a store erase; being built. Stage 5's up-merge (go from Web for the owner): a request ``{:op :sync-up}`` to root's writer at each turn's end, the ONE merge path the turn end, a private session's save (``:seon.agent/save``, acted on by the reconciler) and 1d's bridge all call; held keys are an open unmerged entry of this agent's or a key a stored red verdict reaches (stored verdicts only, so bin/land's greens are reused); holds are entities (``:seon.hold/*``, a content id of [agent key reason]); the automatic merge of a green task is deleted (reconcile's octopus path, the writer's octopus-merge and judged-members; the gate's :merge-candidate stays); about +90 -150 to -200; about 1.6M ticks a turn estimated. Being built. Not built: the up-merge at each turn's end with holds as entities, the save fact, deleting the automatic merge of a green task, and re-model 1 step 2 (``seon.agent/a-runs-measures-are-their-own-entities``). The 1d landing bridge becomes an ordinary syncing session under this up-merge (its hold check reuses bin/land's greens by digest), so 1d lands with this branch. Measured: one agent run wrote 432 datoms in 33 transactions (about 65 a turn); merges stay flat in main's size (main 7.6x, cost +3-6%). Landing order (Optimization, agreed with Web, 2026-11-09; docs/plan.md's Now): (0) FIRST, jolt/index-arg with its Jolt :private? pin (``seon.source/analysis-never-writes-the-image``), as soon as the pin is proved: it is independent of the erase window, and 2a, 2b and stage 4 wait on it; (1) the erase window: the config in force as its own entity (``seon.config/the-config-in-force-is-its-own-entity``), Context's message-delivery re-model (no :seon.message/call), a cite is a ref (``seon.ref/a-cite-is-a-ref-to-what-exists``), with one store erase, Web and Context warned first; (2) jolt/sync (stage 5) with jolt/dbboot (1d) merged into it, landed as one, its up-merge reviewed before 1d goes in; (3) jolt/hiccup with stage 4, its consumer, after Web's browser check of the placeholders. Every landing message goes to the Web session (running point) before bin/land.

